--- machine\system\time
canonical: Machine\System\Time

Timed reads this subtree to select time sources, choose the time zone, and
control automatic synchronization. Registry permissions govern policy changes;
the control socket has separate query and control rights.

A failed complete registry read retains the last running configuration.
Explicit reload reports the failure; startup fails if the initial read cannot
complete. Malformed individual settings retain their documented fallback or
warning behavior. Changing the source list restarts measurement.

For manual time, set Automatic to 0 before using clock set. Direct clock changes
while automatic synchronization is active can be corrected or rejected by the
daemon's jump checks. The service's RequiredPrivileges grant is documented
below.

--- machine\system\time servers
canonical: Machine\System\Time Servers
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent; select eligible DHCP sources or built-in fallbacks)
applies: live

The explicit source list has highest precedence. Each entry contains a host,
an optional port, and optional words:

    time.example.org
    time.example.org:4460
    dc01.corp.example prefer
    ntp.lan unauthenticated

`prefer` breaks selection ties; it does not bypass agreement checks.
`unauthenticated`, also spelled `noauth`, selects plain NTP and requires
AllowUnauthenticated. Unknown options invalidate that entry with a warning.

The first available list is selected: valid explicit entries, eligible DHCP
sources, then the built-in fallbacks. Lists are not merged. Authentication
filtering happens after selection; excluding every source in the selected
list does not select the next list.

Fallbacks are time.cloudflare.com, nts.netnod.se, and ptbtime1.ptb.de. TLS
certificates are checked against the configured name, not a name returned by
DNS. Replacing an operator requires configuration or a software update.

Use several independent sources when disagreement detection is needed. One
source provides no independent comparison; two disagreeing sources cannot
establish which is correct.

--- machine\system\time allowunauthenticated
canonical: Machine\System\Time AllowUnauthenticated
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Controls whether plain-NTP sources can be used.

- `0`: require NTS authentication.
- `1`: permit sources explicitly selected as unauthenticated, including
  eligible DHCP sources.

NTS establishes keys over authenticated TLS and authenticates time packets.
Plain NTP checks the echoed request value but cannot authenticate a server
against an observer able to see and alter the exchange.

Failed key exchange can leave the machine unsynchronized. The recorded build
epoch is a bootstrap lower bound, not verified current time, and cannot
guarantee that TLS certificate validation will succeed.

--- machine\system\time usefromdhcp
canonical: Machine\System\Time UseFromDHCP
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Controls use of DHCP option 42 from netd's snapshot when no valid explicit
source list exists. Zero ignores those sources. One selects eligible DHCP
sources as plain NTP; AllowUnauthenticated must also permit them.

Enable this only when the DHCP configuration and network path are suitable
sources of time policy. A configured DHCP address is not an authenticated
server identity.

--- machine\system\time minpoll
canonical: Machine\System\Time MinPoll
type: REG_DWORD
default: 6
applies: live

The shortest interval between polls of one source, expressed as a power of
two seconds. For example, 6 means 64 seconds and 10 means 1,024 seconds.
The daemon clamps the exponent to 4 through 17. Lower values increase server
traffic and the frequency of measurements.

--- machine\system\time maxpoll
canonical: Machine\System\Time MaxPoll
type: REG_DWORD
default: 10
applies: live

The longest polling interval, expressed as a power of two seconds. The daemon
clamps the exponent to 4 through 17 and raises it to MinPoll if needed.
Polling adapts between the configured bounds as synchronization develops.

--- machine\system\time controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Time ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: (absent — the compiled default is used)
applies: live

The self-relative security descriptor for /run/timed/time.sock.
The rights are 0x1 for query and 0x2 for control. The compiled default grants
SYSTEM and Administrators both rights and Everyone query access.

Reload and manual set require control. Registry policy writes are governed
by the registry key's descriptor, independently of this value.

--- machine\system\time automatic
canonical: Machine\System\Time Automatic
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

One enables polling and automatic clock discipline. Manual clock-setting
requests are refused in this mode.

Zero stops polling and permits clock set through the daemon's control right.
The last learned frequency correction remains active. Status reports manual
operation and remains unsynchronized because sources are not being checked.

Re-enabling automatic operation starts measurement again and permits an
initial clock step when sources establish a usable time.

--- machine\system\time timezone
canonical: Machine\System\Time TimeZone
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — UTC)
valid: a zone name from the tzdata package, such as `Europe/London` or `America/New_York`
applies: live

The system time zone, named from the installed tzdata database. Timed copies
the zone file to the pre-created /etc/localtime at startup and when the chosen
zone changes. Files and metadata must be regular files of at most 16 MiB;
symlinks to regular files are supported. The copy is updated in place, so
readers can see an empty or partial copy while a write is in progress.

An absent value selects UTC. Invalid or unavailable names are rejected and
retried after registry changes or ten minutes. Timed records the last applied
name and trusts that record after restart only if its file matches localtime.
An unknown zone is displayed as UTC; that fallback is not proof that the
localtime file contains UTC data.

Programs decide when to reload local-time data. Existing processes are not
guaranteed to change their display immediately. Protocol timestamps remain
UTC; the zone controls local presentation.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\s-1-5-80-2061758774-2413367500-3090050229-3167896262-4120155161 privileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\S-1-5-80-2061758774-2413367500-3090050229-3167896262-4120155161 Privileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: SeSystemtimePrivilege, SeChangeNotifyPrivilege, SeAuditPrivilege
valid: privilege names; see the generic page for the vocabulary
applies: next start

This entry grants privileges to timed's service SID. It does not grant them
to every service running under LocalService. The SID is derived from the
service name TIMED and is also carried on timed's service token.

SeSystemtimePrivilege permits clock adjustment. SeChangeNotifyPrivilege
permits traversal needed to reach service files after peinit restricts the
token to RequiredPrivileges. SeAuditPrivilege permits timed.clock.stepped
events; failed event writes produce warnings without undoing a clock step.

Removing the clock privilege prevents adjustment even if the daemon can
measure offsets. Disable automatic synchronization or the service when a
separate system is responsible for steering the clock.
